 # RadSplat
 ## Overview
-RadSplat initializes 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by converting a dense point cloud derived from a trained Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) into Gaussians [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the topological inconsistencies and sparsity of raw Structure-from-Motion (SfM) point clouds [S1, unofficial]. The method bypasses or drastically shortens the per-scene gradient descent loop required by standard 3DGS [S1, unofficial].
+RadSplat initializes 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by converting a dense point cloud derived from a trained Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) into Gaussians [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It solves the topological inconsistencies and sparsity inherent in raw Structure-from-Motion (SfM) point clouds [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard 3DGS, which relies on iterative per-scene gradient descent as described in [Optimization and Training](17385-optimization-and-training.md), RadSplat bypasses or drastically shortens this optimization loop [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A trained NeRF model for the target scene [S1, S2, unofficial]. Sparse multi-view images are used to train the initial NeRF [S1, unofficial].
-**Outputs:** A set of $N$ 3D Gaussians $\{G_i\}_{i=1}^N$ with optimized position, rotation, scale, opacity, and appearance parameters [S1, S2, unofficial].
-**Data Structures:** The output follows the standard 3DGS primitive layout [G]. Each primitive $i$ stores: Position $\mathbf{p}_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$, Rotation $q_i \in \mathbb{R}^4$ (unit quaternion), Scale $\mathbf{s}_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$, Opacity $\alpha_i \in [0, 1]$, and appearance parameters (e.g., spherical harmonic coefficients) [G].
